## Counterexample at M

The unsigned fixture records two heads of one PR, a finding against the first,
and a code change from X=1 to X=2 before its replay succeeds. The actual
warrant.review.to_replay enumerates both finding/head pairs. The actual replay
runner collects only the second head's observation. It declines the first because
its code differs from the current checkout (warrant/review.py:133-153).
After modeling publication of that unsigned candidate in memory, a second runner
invocation still leaves the original pair due and does not execute it. Independent
unsigned run_test controls give reproduces at the original commit and
does_not_reproduce at the fixed commit.

The waker's real replay/cover/pending state machine, with its publication boundary
simulated, marks the older failed replay covered and has no pending retry
(integrations/warrant-review/waker/waker.py:648-709). The original reproducing
head thus remains without a checker observation. Running again at a newer
records-only tip repeats the skip; it cannot restore the old result.

The #504 disclosure fix DOES work: at the subsequent wake the reviewer is told
which finding has no observation. This probe explicitly asserts that positive
control (F1). It does not repeat the prior claim of a silent skip. F2 records the
remaining failure: disclosure and a later head's result are not replay at the
original head. T's residual explicitly leaves other-head findings to the reviewer
and says partial replays are not retried. That residual describes the missing work;
it does not revise D's requirement that the checker replay the test at each head.

To close D as written, execute the outstanding finding/head pairs at their own
named commits and retain their observations, with a regression in which the old
head reproduces and its successor does not. Alternatively, change the obligation's
scope through its own new version before claiming that narrower work is complete.

## Probe boundary

The script below calls the actual replay runner, to_replay, run_test, unobserved,
and the waker's memo/state/wake methods. Fixture input records use only the public,
deterministic test identities in tests/world.py. The checker receives a public-only
SimpleNamespace with no seed or signing method; repo.file is intercepted and
collects proposed unsigned records. Publication is modeled in memory and at the
waker's _replay boundary. No checker seed is read; no replay attest is signed or
stored; no real GitHub publication, launchd run or sandbox delivery is exercised.
Working files and fixture repositories remain outside the review worktree.

